  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 650 PM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA fairly vigorous mid level wave accompanied by anomalous moisture\ncontinues to lift north across central MT this afternoon ahead of\nan upper level trough tracking east across the Rockies. This will\ncontinue to support widespread precipitation across much of the \narea into early this evening before drying behind the eastward \nmoving trough brings an end to precipitation from west to east \nlate this evening/overnight. \n\nPotential for embedded isolated thunderstorms and brief moderate-\nheavy precipitation rates will decrease late this afternoon as the \nstrongest lift moves out of the area and stability increases. The \nrisk for impacts from localized intense precipitation has also \ndecreased as snow levels continue to lower, leading to reduced \nrunoff. \n\nSurge of colder air from Canada will continue to spread east across \nremaining eastern portions of north-central MT and south through the \nvalleys of SW MT through the rest of this afternoon and evening, \nbringing a transition to wet snow to even the lowest elevations \nbefore precipitation ends later this evening. Strongest north/northwest\nwinds with the initial surge of the cooler airmass across western\nportions of nort-central MT will begin to gradually decrease late\nthis afternoon, while eastern portions of north-central MT will \nhave winds peak late this afternoon but not likely to noticeably \ndiminish until later tonight. \n\nModerate to heavy snowfall rates are most likely to occur late this \nafternoon with lingering anomalous moisture still in place \nahead of the upper trough axis, with a transition to more \norographic/up-slope forced snowfall occurring this evening before \nprecipitation ends. High resolution model ensembles have the \nhighest probabilities(80% or better) for 1\"/hr rated across the \nLittle Belt and N-central MT Island Ranges and nearby foothills \nbetween 4pm and 7pm with a 20-40% probability for adjacent \nportions of Fergus and Judith Basin counties including the Judith\nGap and Lewistown areas. \n\nA drier westerly flow moves across the Northern Rockies Saturday \nbehind the departing system with a broad area of upper level \ntroughing lingering across western Canada and extending to \noffshore of the US West Coast. This holds a cooler than average \nairmass across the region into early next week with surface high \npressure centered over the Canadian Prairies and generally dry \nconditions prevailing through Monday. Medium range models are \nconverging on the idea of a lobe of energy moving out of the \nlarger scale trough across the NW US around Tuesday. This would be\na colder system with lower snow levels to start, but would likely\nnot have as much deep moisture available as the current system \nand there is still considerable uncertainty this this time with \nmore specific details. Hoenisch\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nMain concerns for winter weather impacts through the rest of this \nafternoon and evening are in areas that see the heavier snowfall \nrates in conjunction with windy conditions. This is most likely \nacross areas near the Little Belts/Snowies and adjacent portions of \nJudith Basin, Fergus and southeastern Cascade counties. Hoenisch",
